+The ICH E17 guideline describes regional pooling in multi-regional clinical trials (MRCTs) in terms of the similarity of effect modifier distributions but names no measure, and to our knowledge no distance between regional effect modifier distributions has been evaluated for it. We quantify how two regions' distributions of each candidate effect modifier differ by the Wasserstein-1 ($W_1$) distance in original units. If the conditional average treatment effect is common to all regions, depends only on that modifier and is Lipschitz with clinical slope $L_{\text{clinical}}$, regional average treatment effects differ by at most $\Delta_{\max} = L_{\text{clinical}} W_1$ on a risk- or mean-difference scale; when $L_{\text{clinical}}$ is unknown, each distance is read against the distance at which a sponsor's bound on the slope could, at worst, produce a clinical margin. In a selection simulation (matches supplied), the standardized mean difference stayed at chance up to 2,000 patients per region in some configurations; $W_1$ and the Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS) statistic reached the target (AUC 0.9) in every configuration. For true distances of about \numval{minW1SdS3toS7} to \numval{maxW1SdS3toS7} SD (normal reference; equal $n$), bootstrap coverage from 100 patients per region was \numval{covRangeS3toS7N100up2dpAbs}, below 0.95 beyond Monte Carlo error in \numval{nCellsCovBelow2MCSE} of ten cells (\numval{covExtE6}--\numval{covExtE5} at the application's operating point). In a GUSTO-I-based example, partner rankings on age and systolic blood pressure differed; a null floor marked distances not distinguishable from no difference. The framework quantifies differences; the pooling judgment remains clinical.}
 
 \keywords{multi-regional clinical trial; ICH E17; effect modifier; Wasserstein distance; regional pooling; distributional similarity}
 
