-Study 1 follows the ADEMP structure for simulation studies.\cite{morris2019} Four worlds are simulated, each with regions constructed either from the anchor's own population (true matches) or from a discordant one. Set 1 (Gaussian) places discordance in location and scale; Set 2 (log-normal) in location and in dispersion combined with shape; Set 3 (mixture) in distributional shape alone, every region, matched or discordant, having mean 50 and SD 10. Set 4 (extremes) places a 5\% subgroup in each tail, 40 units from the centre in the anchor; discordant regions displace both subgroups (to 70 or 100 units), raise their prevalence to 12\% (mean fixed), or displace only the upper subgroup to 100 units, and two control regions instead shift their whole population by 2 and 3 units. In the three Set 4 cells in which a subgroup is displaced (symmetric and asymmetric severity) or its prevalence raised, a subgroup of prevalence $\varepsilon$ displaced by $d$ changes the KS statistic by at most about $\varepsilon$ whatever $d$ is, but $W_1$ by $\varepsilon d$, so $W_1$'s sample-size advantage there follows from the construction; the bulk-shift control, a location shift of the whole population, removes it. Ground truth is the construction label, so the target is defined without reference to any of the compared measures and the comparison is not circular.
