+Sharpness and the monotone case. Over the unrestricted Lipschitz class, the supremum $L_{\text{clinical}} W_1$ of equation~(\ref{eq:heterogeneity_bound}) is attained by $\tau^*(x) = L_{\text{clinical}} \int_{x_0}^{x} \operatorname{sgn}(F_2(t) - F_1(t))\,dt$, whose slope changes sign at every crossing of the two CDFs. If $\tau$ is also monotone, with $0 \leq \tau' \leq L_{\text{clinical}}$, then $\bar{\tau}_1 - \bar{\tau}_2 = \int (F_2 - F_1)\,\tau'\,dx$ lies between $-L_{\text{clinical}} A_-$ and $L_{\text{clinical}} A_+$, where $A_\pm = \int (F_2 - F_1)_\pm\,dx$ satisfy $A_+ + A_- = W_1$ and $A_+ - A_- = \mu_1 - \mu_2$, so that
+\begin{equation}
+\sup_{\tau\ \text{monotone},\ L_{\text{clinical}}\text{-Lipschitz}} |\bar{\tau}_1 - \bar{\tau}_2| = L_{\text{clinical}} \max(A_+, A_-) = \tfrac{1}{2} L_{\text{clinical}} \left( W_1 + |\mu_1 - \mu_2| \right),
+\label{eq:mono_bound}
+\end{equation}
+which equals $L_{\text{clinical}} W_1$ when the CDFs do not cross and $L_{\text{clinical}} W_1 / 2$ when the means coincide. In the application of Section~\ref{sec:application}, the factor $(W_1 + |\mu_1 - \mu_2|)/(2 W_1)$ by which a monotone CATE would reduce $\Delta_{\max}$ ranges from \numval{monoRatioAgeMin} to \numval{monoRatioAgeMax} across the age distances and from \numval{monoRatioSbpMin} to \numval{monoRatioSbpMax} across the SBP distances (point estimates, including the unresolved R2 on SBP).
